ale&quot;:[0],&quot;markdown&quot;:[0,&quot;---\n\ntitle: Building with Lat=
tice\nsubtitle: The Lattice platform and the Lattice SDK=E2=84=A2\nhide-fee=
dback: true\n-------------------\n\nThe **Lattice SDK=E2=84=A2** allows dev=
elopers to build applications, data services,\nand hardware integrations wh=
ich create, exploit, and enhance Lattice data.\nThe [**Lattice Developer Pr=
ogram**](https://www.anduril.com/lattice-sdk/) complements this documentati=
on by providing\nqualified developers with access to development environmen=
ts running Lattice\nservices with representative notional data, as well as =
access to Anduril solutions\narchitecture guidance and developer support.\n=
\n&lt;CardGroup cols=3D{3}&gt;\n  &lt;Card title=3D\&quot;Get started\&quot=
; href=3D\&quot;/guides/getting-started/quickstart\&quot;&gt;\n    Publishi=
ng a surface vessel to Lattice.\n  &lt;/Card&gt;\n\n  &lt;Card title=3D\&qu=
ot;API Reference\&quot; href=3D\&quot;/reference/overview/overview\&quot;&g=
t;\n    Learn about the Lattice APIs.\n  &lt;/Card&gt;\n\n  &lt;Card title=
=3D\&quot;Sample apps\&quot; href=3D\&quot;/samples/overview\&quot;&gt;\n  =
  Run sample apps on your local machine.\n  &lt;/Card&gt;\n&lt;/CardGroup&g=
t;\n\n## Data flow\n\nAn *integration* with Lattice can be unidirectional o=
r bidirectional, supporting one or both of the following functions:\n\n* Pu=
shing data into Lattice, such as publishing data feeds or integrating a new=
 robot.\n* Pulling data from Lattice, such as tracks generated by towers to=
 task against them.\n\nThe main entry-points for these integrations are the=
 [`Entities`](/reference/rest/entities/publish-entity),\n[`Tasks`](/referen=
ce/rest/tasks/create-task), and [`Objects`](/reference/rest/objects/list-ob=
jects) APIs.\nThe following shows how your integration fits into the Lattic=
e ecosystem:\n\n&lt;Frame background=3D\&quot;subtle\&quot;&gt;\n  &lt;img =
src=3D\&quot;file:assets/images/diagrams/overview.png\&quot; alt=3D\&quot;D=
iagram showing how your integration fits into the Lattice ecosystem.\&quot;=
 /&gt;\n&lt;/Frame&gt;\n\n## Protocols\n\nLattice supports both **REST** an=
d **gRPC** protocols, each optimized for different integration patterns.\nR=
EST provides familiar HTTP/JSON workflows ideal for web applications and ra=
pid development. gRPC offers\nbinary Protocol Buffers encoding for bandwidt=
h-efficient hardware integrations.\n\nYour protocol choice impacts authenti=
cation patterns, payload sizes, and available APIs. The\n[Objects API](/gui=
des/objects/overview) for file operations is only available via REST, while=
 both\nprotocols provide full access to Entities and Tasks APIs.\n\n&lt;Not=
e icon=3D\&quot;network-wired\&quot; iconPosition=3D\&quot;left\&quot; titl=
e=3D\&quot;Architecture insight\&quot;&gt;\n  Lattice uses gRPC and Protobu=
f internally to efficiently transmit data between Lattice nodes.\n  Protobu=
f's binary encoding reduces bandwidth by 30-50% compared to JSON while main=
taining data\n  integrity across distributed Lattice deployments.\n&lt;/Not=
e&gt;\n\n&lt;CardGroup cols=3D{2}&gt;\n  &lt;Card title=3D\&quot;gRPC\&quot=
; icon=3D\&quot;webhook\&quot; iconPosition=3D\&quot;left\&quot; href=3D\&q=
uot;/reference/grpc\&quot;&gt;\n    Best for hardware integrations publishi=
ng high-frequency telemetry from drones, robots,\n    and sensors. Binary e=
ncoding reduces bandwidth in constrained networks.\n  &lt;/Card&gt;\n\n  &l=
t;Card title=3D\&quot;REST\&quot; icon=3D\&quot;globe\&quot; iconPosition=
=3D\&quot;left\&quot; href=3D\&quot;/reference/rest\&quot;&gt;\n    Best fo=
r web applications, dashboards, and administrative tools. Familiar HTTP pat=
terns\n    accelerate development with standard debugging tools.\n  &lt;/Ca=
rd&gt;\n&lt;/CardGroup&gt;\n\nFor more information, see [Choose a protocol]=
(/guides/best-practices/choose-a-protocol).\n\n## Use cases\n\nThe Lattice =
API and the services that power it are designed to be flexible and support\=
na variety of workflows. The following are some of the common use-cases:\n\=
n&lt;Icon icon=3D\&quot;browser\&quot; size=3D\&quot;8\&quot; /&gt; **Apps*=
*:\n\nLattice apps are user-facing software solutions which consume and gen=
erate Lattice\ndata. A Lattice app provides specialized end-user capabiliti=
es such as command and control,\nsituational awareness, tasking, or plannin=
g.\n\n&lt;Icon icon=3D\&quot;circle-nodes\&quot; size=3D\&quot;8\&quot; /&g=
t; **Integrations**:\n\nLattice integrations facilitate data transfer betwe=
en Lattice and independent,\nexternal systems such as sensors, data feed pr=
oducers, robotics, and other hardware\nand software capabilities. By integr=
ating with Lattice, you increase the impact of\nyour integration by leverag=
ing Anduril's widely adopted framework.\n\n&lt;Icon icon=3D\&quot;database\=
&quot; size=3D\&quot;7\&quot; /&gt; **Data services**:\n\nLattice data serv=
ices are software modules which enrich or modify data within Lattice,\nusua=
lly without directly presenting a front-end workflow to end-users.\nFor ins=
tance, Lattice data services might provide language translation services,\n=
track correlation, generative AI synthesis of track pictures, to name a few=
. Lattice lets you process\ndata continuously or on-demand.\n\n## What's ne=
xt?\n\n* Get started developing with the Lattice using [Lattice Sandboxes](=
/guides/developer-tools/sandboxes).\n* Learn how to [set up your developmen=
t environment](/guides/getting-started/set-up).\n&quot;],&quot;perViewerCop=
